. The third is a hook rule: it outlines a column that refuses the card you are dragging.",[81,1346,1347,1348,1350],{},"A variable set on ",[88,1349,247],{}," applies to that board only. A variable set on one card applies to that card only.",[1352,1353,1355],"h3",{"id":1354},"tailwind-apps","Tailwind apps",[81,1357,1358,1359,1207],{},"Tailwind v4 apps can map variables to utilities with ",[88,1360,1361],{},"@theme inline",[116,1363,1365],{"className":118,"code":1364,"language":120,"meta":121,"style":121},"@theme inline {\n    --color-ff-surface: var(--ff-surface, var(--gray-50));\n    --color-ff-accent: var(--ff-accent, var(--primary-500));\n}\n",[88,1366,1367,1378,1403,1427],{"__ignoreMap":121},[125,1368,1369,1372,1375],{"class":127,"line":128},[125,1370,1371],{"class":131},"@theme",[125,1373,1374],{"class":1217}," inline ",[125,1376,1377],{"class":139},"{\n",[125,1379,1380,1383,1385,1387,1389,1391,1393,1395,1397,1400],{"class":127,"line":164},[125,1381,1382],{"class":1217},"    --color-ff-surface",[125,1384,1207],{"class":139},[125,1386,1224],{"class":1223},[125,1388,1227],{"class":139},[125,1390,291],{"class":1217},[125,1392,140],{"class":139},[125,1394,1224],{"class":1223},[125,1396,1227],{"class":139},[125,1398,1399],{"class":1217},"--gray-50",[125,1401,1402],{"class":139},"));\n",[125,1404,1405,1408,1410,1412,1414,1416,1418,1420,1422,1425],{"class":127,"line":171},[125,1406,1407],{"class":1217},"    --color-ff-accent",[125,1409,1207],{"class":139},[125,1411,1224],{"class":1223},[125,1413,1227],{"class":139},[125,1415,435],{"class":1217},[125,1417,140],{"class":139},[125,1419,1224],{"class":1223},[125,1421,1227],{"class":139},[125,1423,1424],{"class":1217},"--primary-500",[125,1426,1402],{"class":139},[125,1428,1429],{"class":127,"line":187},[125,1430,197],{"class":139},[81,1432,1433,1434,1436,1437,1439,1440,1443],{},"Flowforge never sets ",[88,1435,239],{}," variables, so each mapped variable needs the fallback shown, which matches the light default in the variable table. Set the variable under ",[88,1438,254],{}," for a dark value. ",[88,1441,1442],{},"inline"," keeps each utility reading the variable at use time, so your overrides still apply.",[1352,1445,1447],{"id":1446},"card-components","Card components",[81,1449,1450,244,1453,244,1455,808,1457,1459,1460,1463,1464,1467,1468,178],{},[88,1451,1452],{},"CardTitle",[88,1454,1031],{},[88,1456,1044],{},[88,1458,1057],{}," are styled by the Modern stylesheet and designed for Modern boards. On a classic board they render ",[88,1461,1462],{},"ff-card-*"," markup, and their look is not guaranteed: after a ",[88,1465,1466],{},"wire:navigate"," visit to a Modern board the Modern stylesheet stays in the page head and can style them. See ",[223,1469,1471],{"href":1470},"\u002Fessentials\u002Fcustomization#card-components","Card Components",[93,1473,226],{"id":1474},"properties-set-with-important",[81,1476,1477,1478,1480,1481,1483,1484,1486,1487,1489],{},"Flowforge sets these properties with ",[88,1479,220],{},", to beat Filament component styles that outrank its sublayer. To override one, put your own ",[88,1482,220],{}," rule in an earlier layer, because cascade layers reverse the order for ",[88,1485,220],{},". An unlayered or later-layer ",[88,1488,220],{}," rule loses to flowforge's layered one.",[116,1491,1493],{"className":118,"code":1492,"language":120,"meta":121,"style":121},"@layer base {\n    .ff-card-meta .fi-badge { color: red !important; }\n}\n",[88,1494,1495,1503,1531],{"__ignoreMap":121},[125,1496,1497,1499,1501],{"class":127,"line":128},[125,1498,132],{"class":131},[125,1500,148],{"class":135},[125,1502,184],{"class":139},[125,1504,1505,1508,1510,1512,1515,1517,1520,1522,1525,1527,1529],{"class":127,"line":164},[125,1506,1507],{"class":139},"    .",[125,1509,1038],{"class":1265},[125,1511,1314],{"class":139},[125,1513,1514],{"class":1265},"fi-badge",[125,1516,1214],{"class":139},[125,1518,1519],{"class":1321}," color",[125,1521,1207],{"class":139},[125,1523,1524],